<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tiến quân ca</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1944">1944</a>; published November <a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Tiến quân ca&quot; (&quot;The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of the Marching Troops&quot;) is the national anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Văn Cao wrote the words and composed the music of the march in 1944, drawing its words and title from his earlier work &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thăng Long</a>&quot; (a former name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>). The words went through many changes in their early stages; Văn Cao revised several lines after many suggestions, toning down the fiercest of them.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Văn Cao wrote and composed the march in 1944, basing its words and title on his earlier work <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thăng Long</a>, a former name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>. The text changed repeatedly from the early 1940s. The first line, Đoàn quân Việt Nam đi, meaning The Vietnamese army marches, was originally Đoàn quân Việt Minh đi, The Viet Minh army marches. The sixth part was first a vow to tear apart the enemy and drink their blood, expressing his anger at the colonialists for letting two million Vietnamese people perish in the famine of 1945. After many suggestions he changed it to a line about fighting for the people until the end. The closing line was published as Nước non Việt Nam ta vững bền.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The anthem has two verses, but usually only the first is sung. After the composer had let Vũ Quý try it, it was published in the papers in November 1944, with lithographs by Văn Cao. On 17 August 1945 it was sung for the first time at a rally in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>. The poet and musician Nguyễn Đình Thi, moved by hearing Văn Cao sing it, wrote Diệt Phát Xít, while Văn Cao wrote Chiến Sĩ Việt Nam; both are still sung.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Hồ Chí Minh approved the song as the anthem of the new Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> on 17 August 1945, and a Liberation Army band played it at Ba Đình Square on 2 September 1945, the day independence was declared. The first National Assembly recognised it officially in 1946, and the first constitution of the republic named it in its third article. In 1955 the Assembly invited authors to edit it again; Văn Cao regretted that the heroic spirit of the song had been lost in the editing. After reunification in 1976 it became the anthem of the Socialist Republic of Vietnam. A contest for a new anthem opened in 1981 but no result was ever announced.</p><p>In 2015 the composer's family registered the song for royalties, drawing protest from veteran musicians, and the Ministry of Culture told the copyright agency to stop collecting. In July 2016 the family donated the song to the nation at a ceremony in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tiến quân ca” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
